-1.1 By [F1], $D$ is simply connected, $S=D/H$ is finite-type cofinite, and $D$ is a FULL ordinary component for $H$. In particular $H$ is nonelementary. If $H=\Gamma$, it is already GF; only the final hull comparison will be needed in this branch. Otherwise choose $g\in\Gamma\setminus H$. The distinct ambient ordinary component $gD$ is disjoint from $D$. Since $\Omega_\Gamma\subset\Omega_H$, it lies in another actual component $E$ of $\Omega_H$. By [F2], $E$ is simply connected and $H$-invariant, $Y=E/H$ is cofinite finite-type, and $S,Y$ have the same primitive cusp classes. Both inclusions into $W=(\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H)/H$ induce group isomorphisms. Their compact cusp truncations have the same genus: for noncompact surfaces use the free rank $2g+n-1$ and the cusp bijection; for closed surfaces use the rank $2g$ of the abelianization. They have negative Euler characteristic. [A1, F1, F2, given, construct, algebra]
+1.1 First derive nonelementarity rather than assume the premise of [F1]'s cofinite conclusion. Its unconditional truncation gives $S=D/H$ as a compact Riemann surface minus finitely many points. A loop in $D$ projects to a loop with trivial monodromy; injectivity makes it nullhomotopic in $S$, and lifting that nullhomotopy contracts the original loop. Thus $D$ is simply connected. The image of monodromy is ALL of $H$: connect a point of $D$ to its translate inside the connected $D$. Since the ambient nonelementary limit set has at least three points, $D$ is a proper plane domain after moving one omitted point to infinity. Every finite complex cycle decomposes into finitely many loops, whose contractions in $D$ leave zero winding about each omitted point; hence this plane domain is homologically simply connected as required by Riemann mapping in [F1]. It gives a disk uniformizer, without any hypothesis on $H$. Transport its free proper deck action to a discrete torsion-free disk group $H_0\cong H$ and give $S$ the descended Poincare metric. It is complete by lifting and extending disk geodesics. Its area is finite directly: a puncture coordinate $0<|w|<R$ has universal upper-half-plane cover; lift its inclusion to the disk uniformizer. Schwarz--Pick in [F1] bounds the density on that punctured disk by $|dw|/(|w|\log(R/|w|))$, whose area on $|w|<R_0<R$ is at most $2\pi/\log(R/R_0)$. There are finitely many punctures and the remaining compact core has finite area in finitely many smooth metric charts. Now a torsion-free elementary discrete disk group is trivial or cyclic: its nonidentity elements have a common axis or parabolic fixed point; an axis reversal has order two, and a dilation together with a nontrivial translation would give arbitrarily small conjugate translations. Discreteness makes the remaining translation subgroup cyclic. The trivial quotient is the disk of infinite area. For a hyperbolic cyclic group of axis length $\ell>0$, Fermi coordinates give area $\int_0^\ell\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\cosh r\,dr\,ds=\infty$. For a parabolic cyclic group $z\mapsto z+\ell$ the fundamental strip has area $\int_0^\ell\int_0^1 y^{-2}\,dy\,dx=\infty$. All elementary cases contradict the finite area just proved. Therefore $H_0$ is nonelementary and nonabelian. The isomorphic $H$ is nonabelian, so it too is nonelementary: every torsion-free discrete elementary sphere group is abelian by the same axis/translation classification. Apply [F1]'s now justified full conclusion: $S$ is cofinite finite-type, with its primitive puncture data, and $D$ is a FULL ordinary component for $H$. If $H=\Gamma$, it is already GF; only the final hull comparison will be needed in this branch. Otherwise choose $g\in\Gamma\setminus H$. The distinct ambient ordinary component $gD$ is disjoint from $D$. Since $\Omega_\Gamma\subset\Omega_H$, it lies in another actual component $E$ of $\Omega_H$. By [F2], $E$ is simply connected and $H$-invariant, $Y=E/H$ is cofinite finite-type, and $S,Y$ have the same primitive cusp classes. Both inclusions into $W=(\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H)/H$ induce group isomorphisms. Their compact cusp truncations have the same genus: for noncompact surfaces use the free rank $2g+n-1$ and the cusp bijection; for closed surfaces use the rank $2g$ of the abelianization. They have negative Euler characteristic. [A1, F1, F2, given, construct, algebra]
 
 2.1 If there are punctures, choose one smooth cusp rim in $Y$ for each of its finitely many punctures. Apply [F2]'s simultaneous annulus construction to obtain disjoint annuli to $S$. Each partner on $S$ has the corresponding peripheral class, by the primitive cusp bijection. The finitely many distinct peripheral partners can be made the rims of disjoint cusp truncation collars on $S$ by their surface annulus parameters; extend those finite collar adjustments to the annuli. In the finite cut comparison, cutting $S$ at such a peripheral rim leaves its cusp-tail annulus and its core side, whose group is ALL of $H$. The corresponding outside cut piece therefore surjects to $H$. The two rims are on different conformal boundary components and are not homotopic there. Thus [F3] applies to every annulus. It gives the two actual puncture tails and the canonical tube for each primitive class. Choose the finite family of tubes disjoint and sufficiently far into their paired sides. The original cylinders' compact initial parts do not change their labels. Write $S_0,Y_0$ for the compact surfaces left after removing these canonical ordinary tails, and $A_i$ for the tube-frontier annuli. If there are no punctures put $S_0=S$, $Y_0=Y$ and use no tubes. Let $W'$ be what remains of $W$ after deleting the open ends of these canonical tubes, retaining $A_i$. It is a connected manifold with corners. The polar tube formula gives a strong deformation retraction $W\to W'$ by $r\mapsto(1-s)r+sR$, fixed at $r=R$ and outside the tubes. Consequently $\pi_1(W')=H$ and its universal cover is contractible: lift this deformation to the contractible cover $\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H$. No compactness of $W'$ has yet been assumed. [F2, F3, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
 
